meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base
==========================

This layer contains the graphics-stack-independent RZ/G3L fast-boot work:

  Phase 1   Device-tree pruning and Linux kernel configuration trimming.
  Phase 2   Rootfs slimming and systemd startup-order optimisation.
  Phase 2b  Removal of the three remaining blocks on the boot critical path,
            each one located with systemd-analyze rather than guessed at.

It intentionally keeps the BSP's Weston graphics stack. Build
`core-image-weston` with the `rz-vlp-fast` distro to reproduce the baseline.
The separate `meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-qt-eglfs` layer adds phase 3 and replaces
Weston with Qt6/EGLFS.

Serial console silencing and bootdelay are U-Boot environment settings and are
not baked into either layer. See section V.

Phase 2b is documented in section VII, including how each figure was measured
and what to re-measure after a change.


I. What this layer changes
==========================

  Distro (conf/distro/rz-vlp-fast.conf)
    - derives from rz-vlp and pins linux-renesas 6.12
    - removes unused DISTRO_FEATURES and MACHINE_FEATURES
    - removes the RZ/VLP development and benchmark package groups
    - disables autostart for services whose features were removed
    - fixes SERIAL_CONSOLES to use ttySC3

  Kernel (recipes-kernel/linux/)
    - the DT patch disables unused network, USB, PCIe, CAN, camera, audio,
      extra UART, RTC and related peripheral nodes
    - fastboot.cfg removes unused kernel subsystems and drivers while keeping
      the display, boot storage and thermal-protection paths
    - fastboot.cfg also drops CONFIG_LEGACY_PTYS and the wireless subsystem,
      which exist only as device units for udev and systemd to walk (VII.1)

  Userspace
    - fastboot-mask-units.bbclass masks vconsole setup and the invalid ttyS0
      getty during rootfs construction
    - systemd-conf_%.bbappend keeps journald in RAM and stops serial forwarding
    - weston-init.bbappend lets Weston start in parallel with multi-user.target


II. Required layers
===================

Use a standard RZ/G3L VLP project with at least:

  poky/meta
  poky/meta-poky
  poky/meta-yocto-bsp
  meta-openembedded/meta-oe
  meta-openembedded/meta-python
  meta-openembedded/meta-multimedia
  meta-arm/meta-arm-toolchain
  meta-arm/meta-arm
  meta-renesas/meta-rz-bsp
  meta-renesas/meta-rz-distro
  meta-rz-features/meta-rz-codecs
  meta-rz-features/meta-rz-graphics
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base

This layer has priority 20 and must follow the Renesas BSP, distro and graphics
layers in bblayers.conf.

Tested with Yocto scarthgap, RZ/G3L BSP v1.0.0-rc1 and linux-renesas
6.12.46-cip8.


III. Build the phase-2 Weston baseline
======================================

  $ source poky/oe-init-build-env build
  $ bitbake-layers add-layer ../meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base

Set in conf/local.conf:

  MACHINE = "smarc-rzg3l"
  DISTRO  = "rz-vlp-fast"

Then build:

  $ bitbake core-image-weston

Useful metadata checks:

  $ bitbake-layers show-layers
  $ bitbake-layers show-appends
  $ bitbake -e core-image-weston | \
        grep -E '^(DISTRO|PREFERRED_VERSION_linux-renesas|SERIAL_CONSOLES)='


IV. Add the Qt/EGLFS phase
==========================

Do not copy the base files into another layer. Add the overlay after this layer:

  meta-qt6
  meta-rz-qt6
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-qt-eglfs

Then select:

  DISTRO = "rz-vlp-fast-qt-eglfs"

and build:

  $ bitbake core-image-hmi


V. U-Boot environment
=====================

The stock bootcmd resets the environment before loading the image:

  bootcmd = env default -a; run bootcmd_check; run bootimage

Remove that reset before saving fast-boot settings:

  => setenv bootcmd 'run bootcmd_check; run bootimage'
  => setenv bootdelay 0
  => setenv prodemmcbootargs 'setenv bootargs rw rootwait root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 quiet loglevel=4 systemd.show_status=0'
  => setenv prodsdbootargs  'setenv bootargs rw rootwait root=/dev/mmcblk1p2 quiet loglevel=4 systemd.show_status=0'
  => saveenv
  => reset

Dropping earlycon avoids replaying the kernel ring buffer synchronously over
the 115200-baud UART. Re-add earlycon when debugging an early kernel failure.


VI. Product constraints
=======================

The DT profile disables Ethernet, USB, PCIe, CAN, camera, audio, i2c0/i2c1/
i2c3, extra UARTs and RTC. Restore both the DT nodes and their matching kernel
and userspace features before adding any of those product functions.

The microSD controller stays enabled for the development boot loop. A fixed
eMMC product can disable it after manufacturing and recovery requirements are
settled.


VII. Phase 2b: the boot critical path
=====================================

Phase 2 left userspace at roughly 75% of the boot. `systemd-analyze plot`
showed why: the path from systemd to graphical.target is not a broad parallel
fan-out with some slow services in it, it is six strictly serial segments.
Because they are serial, the savings from removing them add up.

Baseline (RZ/G3L EVK, phase 2 + meta-rzg3l-fast-logo, eMMC boot):

  systemd-analyze -> 553ms (kernel) + 6.284s (userspace) = 6.837s
                     graphical.target reached after 6.176s in userspace

  segment                                    kernel-rel     cost
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  kernel -> systemd runnable                 0     .. 0.553   553 ms
  systemd start-up + sysinit.target          0.553 .. 2.757  2204 ms   VII.2
  basic.target / sockets / user-sessions      2.757 .. 2.883   126 ms
  512 legacy PTY device units                2.883 .. 3.997  1114 ms   VII.1
  getty.target / multi-user.target           3.997 .. 4.069    72 ms
  weston.service                             4.069 .. 6.727  2658 ms   VII.3
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  graphical.target                                   6.729

`systemd-analyze plot` uses a kernel-relative x axis; `critical-chain` prints
userspace-relative `@` times. The two differ by exactly the 553 ms kernel
phase. All figures in this section are kernel-relative, so they line up with
dmesg timestamps.

Do not use the serial `login:` prompt as the metric. serial-getty is Type=idle,
so it deliberately waits for the job queue to drain - in the baseline it prints
at 7.897s, 1.2 s after the boot has actually finished.


VII.1 512 legacy PTY device units - 1114 ms
-------------------------------------------

CONFIG_LEGACY_PTYS creates 256 BSD-style pty pairs. udev emits an event per
device and systemd instantiates two device units for each, so the boot
transaction carries 512 units that nothing will ever open - 81% of every unit
in the plot. systemd walks them serially, and that walk lands squarely between
systemd-user-sessions.service finishing (2.980s) and multi-user.target /
weston.service being allowed to start (4.055s / 4.069s).

The same 512 udev events are also most of the work in
systemd-udev-trigger.service (1.049s), and the reason dev-mmcblk0p2.device
does not settle until 2.757s.

Fixed in fastboot.cfg by dropping CONFIG_LEGACY_PTYS. CONFIG_UNIX98_PTYS
(/dev/ptmx) is asserted positively in the same fragment so a defconfig change
can never leave the board with no pty support at all. The wireless subsystem
goes with it: rz-vlp-fast already removes wifi and bluetooth from
DISTRO_FEATURES, but the kernel still built cfg80211/mac80211 in - visible in
the boot log as the regulatory.db load failing - and rfkill added two more
device units.


VII.4 Re-measuring
------------------

  # on the target
  systemd-analyze
  systemd-analyze blame
  systemd-analyze critical-chain graphical.target
  systemd-analyze plot > /boot.svg

Read the numbers from `systemd-analyze`, not from the serial log. Take ten
boots and record the P95: systemd-random-seed.service blocks for a variable
time (VII.2), so a single best-case boot is not representative.

When measuring anything that logs at info level, remember that
systemd-conf_%.bbappend sets MaxLevelStore=warning - raise it to info for the
measurement boot or the records will not be in the journal.
